## Meta Fields Explanation

| Field | Purpose | When Used |
|-------|---------|-----------|
| `type` | Identifies the resource type and operation | Always |
| `url` | The clean API endpoint called (without query params) | Always |
| `timestamp` | ISO 8601 timestamp of response generation | Always |
| `request_id` | Unique identifier for request tracing and debugging | Always |
| `version` | API version being used | Always |
| `status` | HTTP status code | Always |
| `message` | Human-readable status message | Always |
| `rate_limit` | Rate limiting information and remaining quota | Always |
| `pagination` | Pagination metadata for collection responses | List responses only |

## Rate Limiting

The API implements rate limiting with the following defaults:
- **Limit**: 100 requests per minute per IP
- **Window**: 60 seconds
- **Headers**: Rate limit info included in every response
- **429 Response**: When limit exceeded

### Users
- `GET /users` - Get all users
- `GET /users/:id` - Get user by ID
- `POST /users` - Create new user
- `PUT /users/:id` - Update user
- `DELETE /users/:id` - Delete user

### Posts
- `GET /posts` - Get all posts
- `GET /posts/:id` - Get post by ID
- `POST /posts` - Create new post
- `PUT /posts/:id` - Update post
- `DELETE /posts/:id` - Delete post
- `GET /posts?authorId=:id` - Get posts by author
- `GET /posts?_embed=comments` - Get posts with comments

### Comments
- `GET /comments` - Get all comments
- `GET /comments/:id` - Get comment by ID
- `POST /comments` - Create new comment
- `PUT /comments/:id` - Update comment
- `DELETE /comments/:id` - Delete comment
- `GET /comments?postId=:id` - Get comments by post

### Categories
- `GET /categories` - Get all categories
- `GET /categories/:id` - Get category by ID
- `POST /categories` - Create new category
- `PUT /categories/:id` - Update category
- `DELETE /categories/:id` - Delete category

### Settings
- `GET /settings` - Get application settings
- `PUT /settings` - Update settings

## Query Parameters

JSON Server supports various query parameters:

- `_page` & `_limit` - Pagination
- `_sort` & `_order` - Sorting
- `_embed` - Include related resources
- `_expand` - Include parent resource
- `q` - Full-text search

Examples:
```
GET /posts?_page=1&_limit=10
GET /posts?_sort=createdAt&_order=desc
GET /posts?q=react
GET /users?role=admin
```

## Development

### Generate realistic data

Generate new mock data using Faker.js:

```bash
pnpm nx run <%= mockProjectName %>:generate
```

Or generate and start the server:

```bash
pnpm nx run <%= mockProjectName %>:seed
```

### Direct execution (alternative)

Build and run the server directly:

```bash
cd <%= mockProjectRoot %>
pnpm build
node dist/src/server.js
```

### Clean Build

```bash
pnpm clean && pnpm build
```

### Start the mock server

```bash
pnpm nx serve <%= mockProjectName %>
```

The server will be available at the configured host and port (default: `http://localhost:3001`)

### Configuration Examples

**Run on a different port:**
```bash
PORT=8080 pnpm nx serve <%= mockProjectName %>
```

**Run with debug logging:**
```bash
DEBUG=true pnpm nx serve <%= mockProjectName %>
```

**Run on all network interfaces:**
```bash
HOST=0.0.0.0 pnpm nx serve <%= mockProjectName %>
```

### Module Resolution Issues

If you encounter "Cannot find module" errors, this is likely due to ES module resolution. The package.json scripts include the `--experimental-specifier-resolution=node` flag to handle this.

Alternatively, you can:
1. Use Node.js 18+ which has better ES module support
2. Ensure all import statements use `.js` extensions
3. Check that the TypeScript compilation is generating correct output

### Modify data

Edit the `db.json` file to add, modify, or remove mock data. The server will automatically reload when changes are detected.

## Usage in Frontend

```typescript
// The API service automatically handles the data/meta format
// You'll receive just the data portion in your responses

const API_BASE_URL = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development' 
  ? 'http://localhost:3001'  // or your configured host:port
  : '/api';

// Get all users - returns just the user array
const users = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/users`).then(res => res.json());

// Create a new post - returns just the created post object
const newPost = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/posts`, {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    title: 'New Post',
    content: 'Post content...',
    authorId: 1,
    tags: ['react'],
    isPublished: true
  })
}).then(res => res.json());

// Get posts with comments - returns just the posts array
const postsWithComments = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/posts?_embed=comments`)
  .then(res => res.json());

// Access pagination info (if needed) by making a raw fetch call
const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/users?_page=1&_limit=10`);
const result = await response.json();
console.log('Data:', result.data);
console.log('Pagination:', result.meta.pagination);
```

## CORS

The mock server is configured to allow CORS requests from any origin, making it easy to use during development.

## Data Persistence

Changes made through the API are persisted to the `db.json` file, so your mock data will survive server restarts during development.

## Troubleshooting

### Port Already in Use
If you see an error about the port being in use, either:
1. Change the port in your `.env` file
2. Stop the conflicting service
3. Use a different port: `PORT=3002 nx serve <%= mockProjectName %>`

### Permission Denied
If you get permission errors, try using a port number above 1024 or run with appropriate permissions.

### Package Manager
This project uses pnpm as the package manager. Make sure you have pnpm installed:
```bash
npm install -g pnpm
```

### Configuration Issues
The server validates configuration on startup and will show warnings for invalid values, falling back to defaults when possible.
